FAILURE MODE 17 (proposed BUILD-DISCIPLINE pattern), from /ideate 2026-07-28 human-directed deep-dive. NAME: 'NO WITNESS AT THE DISPATCH BOUNDARY'. SHAPE: the only evidence that a scheduled job ran is the log that job writes about itself. There is no independent record of the DISPATCH -- so a job that never starts leaves no trace anywhere, and 'never scheduled', 'silently unscheduled', 'started and died before its first echo' are all indistinguishable from each other and from 'nothing to report'. VERIFIED TONIGHT, not quoted: '#cron.*' is commented out in /etc/rsyslog.d/50-default.conf, 'journalctl -t CRON --since 2026-07-25' is EMPTY, and there is no /var/log/cron.log. mandark's cron writes nothing. Every 'last scheduled run' figure this ecosystem reports is therefore self-reported by the actor. RELATION TO EXISTING PATTERNS: this is pattern 9 ('the actor grades its own homework') moved one level down -- pattern 9 says the actor must not be the source of truth for WHETHER IT WORKED; this says the actor is currently also the sole source of truth for WHETHER IT RAN, which is strictly worse because a missing log is indistinguishable from a quiet success. It is also the structural reason pattern 1 (silent failure) is so expensive here: there is no outer loop that could notice absence. WHAT IT IS CURRENTLY HIDING, all three found tonight: (1) nine-speakers has produced nothing since 2026-07-21 -- its wrapper and state dir still exist under svc-vaporwave but it is in NO crontab on either account; 7 days of silence, zero alarms. (2) svc-vaporwave's ~/bin still holds crt-nightly-batch-loop.sh and nine-speakers-nightly-batch-loop.sh plus their .local/share state dirs and stale sweep.lock files, both unreferenced by that account's crontab -- pattern 6 cruft, except the shared surface is a shared ACCOUNT rather than a shared host, which BUILD-DISCIPLINE's shared-host-footprint row does not currently name. (3) vkv-inventory's dead-man switch expired at 2026-07-27T20:51:32 and tonight's 04:00 run will do no work; that one IS designed to be loud and will show as expiry, so it is the control case proving the others are not. THE ASK: a dispatch witness written by something that is not the job -- the simplest honest form is re-enabling cron logging (uncomment cron.* in rsyslog, machine-wide config, so notify-senechal applies) plus a heartbeat reconciler that compares 'jobs that should have fired since last check' against 'jobs that wrote anything', and FLAGs the difference. Note the BLIND-status requirement from tonight's earlier .idea drop applies with full force here: a reconciler that finds no crontab entries must exit BLIND, not clean.
